\name{demography_processes}
\alias{demography_processes}
\alias{age_process}
\alias{survival_process}
\alias{staged_survival_process}
\alias{reproduction_process}
\alias{census_process}
\title{Ageing, survival, reproduction and census processes}
\usage{
age_process(name = "age", increment = 1L)

survival_process(
  probability,
  eligible = NULL,
  cause = "natural",
  name = "survival",
  requires = character(),
  params = list()
)

staged_survival_process(
  probability,
  post_share,
  stage = c("pre", "post"),
  field = "survival_draw",
  cause = "natural",
  name = paste0("survival_", stage),
  requires = character(),
  params = list()
)

reproduction_process(
  breeding_probability,
  litter_size,
  offspring_sex,
  mate = NULL,
  require_mate = !is.null(mate),
  newborn_fields = NULL,
  newborn_field_names = character(),
  inherit = character(),
  name = "reproduction",
  requires = character(),
  params = list()
)

census_process(
  metrics = NULL,
  name = "census",
  requires = character(),
  params = list()
)
}
\arguments{
\item{name}{Process name used in events, accounting and error messages.}
\item{increment}{Whole number of age units added per execution.}
\item{probability}{Survival probability over the whole interval: one number, or a function of \code{(pop, state, context, params)} returning one probability per living individual.}
\item{eligible}{Optional function of \code{(pop, state, context, params)} returning one logical value per living individual. \code{NULL} means every living individual.}
\item{cause}{Label stored with the accounting event, for example \code{"natural"} or \code{"harvest"}.}
\item{requires}{Additional state paths (\code{population.<field>} or \code{globals.<name>}) read by the supplied functions; checked before a run starts.}
\item{params}{Named list of modeller parameters, passed unchanged to every supplied function as \code{params}.}
\item{post_share}{Share of the interval's mortality hazard that falls after the mid-interval event, in [0, 1].}
\item{stage}{Which stage this process applies: \code{"pre"} (before the event) or \code{"post"} (after it).}
\item{field}{Name of an unused temporary global shared by the pre and post stages. The post stage removes it. Use distinct names for overlapping pairs of stages.}
\item{breeding_probability}{Probability that a female breeds: one number, or a function of \code{(pop, state, context, params)} returning one probability per living individual (values for males are ignored).}
\item{litter_size}{Function of \code{(mothers, pop, state, context, params)} returning one litter size per breeding female, for example \code{\link{litter_poisson}()}.}
\item{offspring_sex}{Function of \code{(mothers, pop, state, context, params)} returning \code{"F"} or \code{"M"} for each newborn; \code{mothers} holds one row index per newborn. Required: the engine supplies no default sex ratio.}
\item{mate}{Optional function of \code{(pop, state, context, params)} returning, for each living individual, the row of its mate (the father of its offspring) or \code{NA}. See \code{\link{mate_random}()} and \code{\link{mate_from_field}()}.}
\item{require_mate}{Whether a recorded mate is required. Supplying a mate function enables this by default. With mate = NULL, reproduction is female-dominant.}
\item{newborn_fields}{Optional function of \code{(mothers, fathers, pop, state, context, params)} returning a named list of the non-core fields of the newborns. The arguments hold current living row indices. A sire lost since mating has an \code{NA} row and retains his recorded ID in the offspring.}
\item{newborn_field_names}{Names of the fields \code{newborn_fields} returns.}
\item{inherit}{Non-core fields copied from each mother to her offspring.}
\item{metrics}{Optional function of \code{(pop, state, context, params)} returning a named numeric vector. The default reports \code{n_alive}, \code{n_female} and \code{n_male}.}
}
\description{
Reusable demographic processes. All rate functions are vectorised over the living population.
}
\details{
Every function supplied to one process sees the same pre-process population, so survival of one individual cannot depend on a death drawn earlier in the same process, and breeding cannot see newborns of the same process. Random draws are taken in population (identifier) order.

\code{staged_survival_process()} splits mortality around events such as a release. Write \eqn{q} for \code{post_share}. An original resident passes the pre stage when its uniform draw is below \eqn{a = s_{pre}^{1-q}}. Survivors carry the draw divided by \eqn{a} into the post stage, where the threshold is \eqn{s_{post}^q}. With fixed stage-specific rates the joint survival probability is their product. If rates are unchanged the final decision matches a single draw against the annual rate exactly. Entrants between stages draw afresh. A zero post share spares all individuals at the post stage.

A census is an ordinary non-mutating process: its position in the lifecycle defines whether it observes the population before or after breeding, mortality or management.
}
\value{
A \code{pva_process}.
}
\examples{
survival <- survival_process(
  probability = function(pop, state, context, params) ifelse(pop$age == 0, 0.5, 0.9)
)
breeding <- reproduction_process(
  breeding_probability = function(pop, state, context, params) as.numeric(pop$age >= 2),
  litter_size = litter_poisson(1.2),
  offspring_sex = offspring_sex_bernoulli(0.5),
  mate = mate_random(function(pop, state, context, params) pop$age >= 2)
)
scenario <- scenario_spec(
  name = "minimal",
  n_runs = 20, n_steps = 10, seed = 1,
  init_fun = function() pva_population(sex = rep(c("F", "M"), 10), age = 3),
  processes = list(survival, age_process(), breeding, census_process())
)
summarise_pva(run_scenario(scenario), threshold = 10)
}
